If i_max is independent of the i in the outerloop you can try fusing the loops (essentially collapse).  It's something I do often which often gives me a small boost.  I also prefer fusing the loops "by hand" rather than with OpenMP because Visual Studio only supports OpenMP 2.0 which does not have collapse and I want my code to work on Windows and Linux. #pragma omp parallel for reduction(+:pot2body) schedule(dynamic)
for(int n=0; n<(nc2*i_max); n++) {
    int i = n/i_max; //i from outer loop
    int j = n%i_max; //i from inner loop 
    double pottmp_j = ... 
    pot2body += pottmp_j;
} If i_max depends on j then this won't work.  In that case follow Grizzly's advice. But one more thing to you can try.  OpenMP has an overhead.  If i_max is too small then using OpenMP could actually be slower.  If you add an if clause at the end of the pragma then OpenMP will only run if the statement is true.  Like this: const int threshold = ... // smallest value for which OpenMP gives a speedup.
#pragma omp parallel for reduction(+:dev) schedule(dynamic) if(i_max > threshold)

