answers.body:
Numerical integration methods such as Runge-Kutta are sequential methods, where each iteration output is the input to the next iteration. You can't calculate the states in parallel since each state needs the previous state solution as an input. E.g., take these first lines from the loop: x1 = private_x[i];
    y1 = private_y[i];
    z1 = private_z[i]; These are used in your RK4 code to eventually calculate the next states: private_x[i + 1] = private_x[i] + dt / 6.0 * (xK1 + 2.0 * xK2 + 2.0 * xK3 + xK4);
    private_y[i + 1] = private_y[i] + dt / 6.0 * (yK1 + 2.0 * yK2 + 2.0 * yK3 + yK4);
    private_z[i + 1] = private_z[i] + dt / 6.0 * (zK1 + 2.0 * zK2 + 2.0 * zK3 + zK4); But you have no reason to believe that in this parallel computation environment the private_x[i], private_y[i], and private_z[i] are actually computed yet. You are using garbage as input to the calculations, and you get garbage out. You must do the iterations in sequence starting from the first one for the RK numerical schemes (Euler, Modified Euler, RK4, etc.) to work.

