answers.body:
You have a race condition. That is, two threads could collide and do an incorrect update. Suppose, we had two threads (e.g. A and B) and they are examining G->[iA] (with value 10) and G->[iB] (with value 20) respectively. Suppose, these values are both less than the value of minD which has the value 30. So, they both want to update minD and nextIndex at the same time. Obviously, thread A has the [ultimately] better value, but there is nothing to prevent A from setting minD/nextIndex and then B trashing that value. This is because the if test and the set of the new values are not atomic . Otherwise, if they were atomic, A would set the shared values and B would not because its if test would fail [and not do the update] because it would be guaranteed to see the values updated by A Caveat: I'm not an openmp guru, so take all of the following with a grain of salt. This should work but may not be super efficient: long
getNextNode(graph * G)
{
    long i;
    long minD;
    long nextNode;

    nextNode = -1;
    minD = INF;

    // Find unvisited node with lowest Distance to the intial node
#pragma omp parallel for shared(minD,nextNode) schedule(runtime)
    for (i = 0; i < G->N; i++) {
        if (G->visited[i] != NOT_VISITED)
            continue;

#pragma omp atomic
        if (G->D[i] < minD) {
            minD = G->D[i];
            nextNode = i;
        }
    }

    return nextNode;
} Another way would to add a reduction clause but I'm not sure that applies because you have two variables.  For example, if you didn't need nextNode , to get the minimum value, you'd could do: long
getNextNode(graph * G)
{
    long i;
    long minD;

    minD = INF;

    // Find unvisited node with lowest Distance to the intial node
#pragma omp parallel for reduction(min:minD) schedule(runtime)
    for (i = 0; i < G->N; i++) {
        if (G->visited[i] != NOT_VISITED)
            continue;

        if (G->D[i] < minD)
            minD = G->D[i];
    }

    return minD;
} Another way is to let each thread's loop free run with private variables (e.g. private(nextNode,minD) ) but store the final nextNode and minD values in a global array, indexed by thread number: struct res {
    long res_node;
    long res_min;
};

struct res thread_list[NUMTHREADS];

thread_list[my_thread_number].res_node = nextNode;
thread_list[my_thread_number].res_min = minD; Then, after exiting the parallel section, traverse the list in the main thread and select the entry with the lowest res_min value. I'm not sure of the exact way to do this under openmp , so the above is pseudo-code, but there are examples of how to do this on the web. But, this strikes me as running in O(n^2) time. That is, getNextNode takes O(n) time, but it is [presumably] called n times (i.e.) O(n*n) or O(n^2) . Assume you have 8 cores, this reduces to O(n^2)/8 , which is still O(n^2) If possible , it might be better to create a sorted list within your graph (e.g. add a long *S to your graph struct) and pre-sort this based on visited/D . The sort would take O(n*log2(n)) time. Then, getNextNode could just traverse S in order and get the same effect. But, the time becomes O(n*log2(n)) + O(n) which reduces to O(n*log2(n)) Of course, it depends whether you modify your graph between calls to getNextNode or not. If you are able to do this, running single threaded might be the way to go.

